  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 116 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge will continue to shift westward mid week and \nposition itself over the Southwestern U.S, thus opening the door \nfor a series of disturbances to track southward across the area. \nThis will provide us with a brief stint of northwest flow aloft \nand the possibility for isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms Thursday, and perhaps again on Friday. Any \nconvection that does manage to develop will likely be high-based \nand would pose a gusty wind threat in any of the stronger cells.\n\nWith the cloud cover in place highs on Thursday will likely be \nslightly cooler and more around the 100 degree mark thus keeping \nus below advisory criteria. However, dewpoints are expected to \nincrease as we remain in southeasterly flow at the surface. \nAdditionally, any areas that receive rain/storms will likely have\nan uptick in humidity as well. Thursday may need to be watched if\nstorms don't pan out as we could easily hit advisory criteria \nonce again this time with the heat indices or feels like \ntemperatures approaching and even exceeding 108 degrees. Friday \nthe ridge looks to migrate ever so slowly eastward and be \npositioned over west TX also weakening in the process. However we \narent expecting temperatures nearly as hot as this past weekend as\nthis ridge should be weaker aloft. Additionally, we could see our\ntypical seabreeze kick off showers and storms across the Coastal \nPlains due to this weaker ridge located overhead. \n\nThis weekend is when things get a bit more murky as the ridge will \nbe continuing its progression eastward but flattening/broadening out \nas it does so. Models are struggling on the placement of this ridge \nas they typically do in weaker scenarios thus we still are expecting \nhot temperatures however they should be closer to around 100 \ndegrees. Similarly the afternoon seabreeze should help to kick \noff isolated showers and a few rumbles of thunder across the \nCoastal plains as we resume our typical summer pattern. Looking \nahead into early next week temperatures look to remain hot but \njust under heat Advisory criteria.",
